ridis\",\"categoricalCS\":\"Category10\",\"eventInputId\":null,\"editionMode\":\"EditionOff\",\"controlWidgets\":false,\"cssRules\":null,\"sliderPosition\":null},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n# Learning Reflection\n\nIn this exercise, I learned that a parallel coordinates plot is the natural choice when I need to compare more than two continuous variables together. The key is **scaling** - using `uniminmax` puts every axis on a common 0-1 range so a variable with large values does not visually swamp the others. `GGally::ggparcoord()` with grouping, a boxplot overlay and region facets exposes the multivariate profile of each region, while `parallelPlot` makes it interactive, letting me brush lines and add per-axis histograms to inspect distributions.\n",
